{
  "requirement": "This project's `mdbook build` and `mdbook serve` generate workspace Rustdoc\nas part of preprocessing, including on the first build in a fresh checkout.\nThe published documentation includes both the book and that generated Rustdoc\nsite in the same GitHub Pages deployment under\n{cite}`requirements.integration.ci`. The appendix contains a \"Rustdocs\" entry\nopening the `trust_but_verify` crate's documentation under\n{cite}`requirements.index.rustdocs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: Structural obligations in `requirements.index.rustdocs.publication` and the roots that cover them:\n\n1. \"`mdbook build` and `mdbook serve` generate workspace Rustdoc as part of preprocessing, including on the first build in a fresh checkout.\" This needs the preprocessor to run Cargo itself. An alternative that keeps the output but violates the clause is a separate manual `cargo doc` step before `mdbook build`. The book would then fail on a fresh checkout. `book.toml#table[preprocessor.habeas.rustdocs]/pair[build-workspace]` (`build-workspace = true`) is the root for this. It sits beside `path = \"target/doc\"` and `crate = \"trust_but_verify\"`.\n\n2. \"The published documentation includes both the book and that generated Rustdoc site in the same GitHub Pages deployment.\" `.github/workflows/ci.yml#pair[jobs]/pair[test-and-build]/pair[steps]/item@52e5491e206d#11` (`run: mdbook build`) produces the `book/` output that contains `rustdocs/`. The later `actions/upload-pages-artifact` step with `path: book` and the `deploy` job publish that one directory. Both are already marked `requirements.integration.ci` on `pair[jobs]/pair[test-and-build]` and `pair[jobs]/pair[deploy]`. The text only cites that requirement for the deployment mechanics, so the deployment obligation is covered by it. The `mdbook build` step marked here is the root that ties the Rustdoc generation to the published build.\n\n3. \"The appendix contains a 'Rustdocs' entry opening the `trust_but_verify` crate's documentation.\" `md/SUMMARY.md#section[Appendix]/paragraph@b6712f736f08` is `[Rustdocs](./rustdocs.md)`. `md/rustdocs.md` contains the standalone `:::{rustdocs}` directive. The `crate` setting selects `trust_but_verify`. Removing the entry or pointing it at another chapter would violate the clause, so the root is needed and present.\n\nThe remaining wording, \"including on the first build in a fresh checkout\", describes behavior. It follows from the `build-workspace` setting and needs no further structural evidence.\n\nThe three supplied roots cover every structural obligation. I found no gap to report.\n\nbehavioral: Obligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n1. **Workspace Rustdoc is generated during preprocessing, including on a first build in a fresh checkout.**\n   - `tests/rustdocs.rs#fn[rustdocs_workspace_build_generates_and_refreshes_the_import]` starts from a fresh temporary project with no `target` directory. It sets `build-workspace = true` and calls `habeas::rustdocs::preprocess`.\n   - It asserts that `fn.first.html` is imported for the root crate and that `member/fn.marker.html` is imported for a workspace member.\n   - It asserts that an excluded dependency is not imported.\n   - It asserts that a later source change produces `fn.second.html`, so generation runs on each build.\n   - It asserts the failure paths: a `path` that doesn't end in `doc`, a failed `cargo doc` (with no `Cargo.lock` created), and a broken source file that keeps the previous import.\n   - `mdbook build` and `mdbook serve` both run the same preprocessor, and the text doesn't distinguish them. Testing the preprocessor entry point therefore covers both.\n\n2. **The book and the generated Rustdoc ship in the same GitHub Pages deployment.**\n   - `tests/rustdocs.rs#fn[rustdocs_ci_publishes_the_book_with_workspace_generation_enabled]` parses `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. It asserts that the `mdbook build` step comes before `actions/upload-pages-artifact@v4`, and that the uploaded path is `book`.\n   - It asserts that `book.toml` sets `build-workspace = true`, `crate = \"trust_but_verify\"` and `path = \"target/doc\"`. It also asserts the `output.habeas` command.\n   - The imported site is placed under the book output. That behavior belongs to `requirements.index.rustdocs`, which the first test also covers. Together these checks tie the single uploaded `book` artifact to the generated Rustdoc.\n\n3. **The appendix has a \"Rustdocs\" entry that opens the `trust_but_verify` documentation.**\n   - The CI test asserts that `md/SUMMARY.md` has `[Rustdocs](./rustdocs.md)` after `# Appendix`.\n   - It asserts that `md/rustdocs.md` contains exactly the standalone `:::{rustdocs}` / `:::` directive.\n   - It asserts that the configured crate is `trust_but_verify`.\n\nThe cited `requirements.integration.ci` and `requirements.index.rustdocs` are context only, and I did not reassess their tests. Both tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. I found no unmarked relevant test and no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "4af4b9c6372aaa942fc8921bd7cda0a2bbb3c56c4cb58df70915cfa3ea1eff8a",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.index.rustdocs.publication",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.index.rustdocs.publication"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} rustdocs.publication",
        "This project's `mdbook build` and `mdbook serve` generate workspace Rustdoc",
        "as part of preprocessing, including on the first build in a fresh checkout.",
        "The published documentation includes both the book and that generated Rustdoc",
        "site in the same GitHub Pages deployment under",
        "{cite}`requirements.integration.ci`. The appendix contains a \"Rustdocs\" entry",
        "opening the `trust_but_verify` crate's documentation under",
        "{cite}`requirements.index.rustdocs`.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.integration.ci",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.integration.ci"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} ci",
        "A GitHub Actions CI workflow MUST run, for pull requests and pushes to `main`, these checks: `cargo habeas coverage -- --locked --workspace` under {cite}`requirements.test-coverage.run`, `cargo test --locked --doc --workspace`, `mdbook test`, the autofix controller tests with `node --test .github/scripts/autofix.test.mjs`, and, in a separate job, the Symposium session-start hook smoke test `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py`. It also builds the book as documentation under {cite}`requirements.index.render_only`, without requiring collected execution reports for rendering. On a push to `main`, only after every check job passes, it deploys the generated `book/` directory to GitHub Pages. The book MUST set `output.html.site-url` to `/habeas-codicem/` for project-site hosting.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.index.rustdocs",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.index.rustdocs"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} rustdocs",
        "A book can import a generated Rustdoc site through its Habeas preprocessor",
        "configuration:",
        "",
        "```toml",
        "[preprocessor.habeas.rustdocs]",
        "path = \"target/doc\"",
        "crate = \"trust_but_verify\"",
        "```",
        "",
        "`path` identifies the generated documentation directory relative to the project",
        "root; `crate` selects the landing page at `<crate>/index.html`. A chapter",
        "consisting of one closed, standalone `{rustdocs}` directive with an empty body",
        "becomes a navigation entry into that crate's Rustdoc site. The directive opens",
        "with at least three colons and closes with at least as many:",
        "",
        "```text",
        ":::{rustdocs}",
        ":::",
        "```",
        "",
        "The built book includes the imported site under `rustdocs/`, preserving its",
        "pages, assets, search data, and links. Selecting the chapter opens the native",
        "Rustdoc page in the same tab, with Rustdoc's own layout and navigation. Relative",
        "links from book chapters to imported item pages resolve when the publication is",
        "hosted under a URL prefix.",
        "",
        "Set `build-workspace = true` to run `cargo doc --locked --workspace --no-deps`",
        "from the book root before each import. In this mode `path` ends in `doc` and",
        "its parent is Cargo's target directory. Cargo reuses current build artifacts.",
        "Omitting the option imports an already generated site.",
        "",
        "The build reports a readable error if the directive has no import configuration, the",
        "configured directory, crate landing page, or any imported site file cannot be",
        "read, the import fails, or an imported file would overwrite a book output file.",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
